-- title:
--   Uniqueness of the exponent in a p-power kernel degree
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and $B$ a nonzero commutative ring, let $\varphi = (\varphi_1,\varphi_2)$ be a pair of power series in two variables over $B$ (an element of `Series B`, i.e. a family `Fin 2 → MvPowerSeries (Fin 2) B`), and let $h,h'$ be natural numbers. Assume that `FormalODModule.HasKernelOfDegree` holds for $\varphi$ both with degree $p^{h}$ and with degree $p^{h'}$; by definition each of these assumptions says that the quotient algebra $B[\![x,y]\!]/(\varphi_1,\varphi_2)$ — the quotient of `MvPowerSeries (Fin 2) B` by the ideal generated by the range of $\varphi$ — is a finite $B$-module, is a projective $B$-module, and that for every field $\kappa$ and every ring homomorphism $f : B \to \kappa$ the $\kappa$-dimension of $\kappa[\![x,y]\!]/(f\varphi_1, f\varphi_2)$, formed from the coefficientwise image of $\varphi$ under $f$, equals the degree in question. The conclusion is that $h = h'$. The proof uses only the fibre-dimension clauses of the two hypotheses, not the finiteness or projectivity clauses.
--
--   This is the well-definedness of the exponent (the height) in a $p$-power degree for the kernel of a map of two-variable formal power series, used so that the height attached to an isogeny of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules depends only on the map and not on a chosen presentation. It is cited in the height computations for formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules and in the rigidification comparisons for fake elliptic curves on the Čerednik–Drinfeld side of the development.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CerednikDrinfeld_FormalODModule_HasKernelOfDegree_eq_of_pow_of_pow.lean

